---
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import ListItem from "../components/ListItem.astro";
import Panel from "../components/Panel.astro";
---

<Panel>
    <h1>Database Skills</h1>
    <p>4+ years professional experience. Click each for more details.</p>
    <ListItem title="Change Data Capture/GoldenGate historical data pipeline">
        Co-lead and co-designed a sophisticated CDC pipeline which captured the
        full change history of 31 replicated tables (i.e. reliable historical
        data that would otherwise be inaccessible to the company) and also
        provided efficient access to current-state data. To achive this, I
        designed a system of partial indices, views, and triggers using advisory
        locks. This system improves on both our prior ideas of separate
        historical and current tables (which opened the door to data
        discrepancies), TIBCO-powered message queues (which was vulnerable to
        data loss), and unique indices (which, unlike advisory locks, caused
        GoldenGate to not use BATCHSQL.) I also designed an automated validation
        processor to give confidence that the new data pipeline's data was
        indeed correct so that we could deploy to prod confidently. Compared to
        the TIBCO approach, delivering this CDC pipeline resulted in a decrease
        of row discrepancy rate in our tables from up to 10% to 0% (perfect
        match).</ListItem
    >
    <ListItem
        title="PostgreSQL internals"
        subtitle="(MVCC system columns (ctid, xmin, xmax), B+
            tree index design)"
        >In order to monitor the in-progress, pre-commit status of a massive 2
        billion row data migration, I studied Postgres MVCC design in order to
        randomly sample the table's internal system columns (ctid, xmin, xmax)
        to efficiently estimate the approximate completion percentage. Moreover,
        in order to better understand <Code>EXPLAIN</Code> output, I read up on the
        design of B+ trees and bitmaps.
    </ListItem>
    <ListItem
        title="Schema design"
        subtitle="(incl. triggers, partial indices, views, etc)"
        >I designed a system of table triggers, enums, and composite partial
        indices to allow for the consolidation of historical audit data and
        efficient current-data access, which allowed our team to confidently
        start owning core data previously kept in the legacy mainframe.
    </ListItem>
    <ListItem
        title="Query design"
        subtitle="(incl. windowing functions, XML/JSON functions, CTEs, etc."
    >
        I continuously researched opportunities for improving and optimizing our
        queries; for example, I redesigned a deduplication process to take only
        7 minutes per table instead of 1 day per table (using <Code
            >DELETE USING</Code
        > instead of a large CTE). I also co-started a new pattern of using <Code
            >DISTINCT ON</Code
        > instead of <Code>RANK()</Code> and <Code>PARTITION</Code> in historical
        point-in-time queries in order to achieve the same result more readably with
        less room for error.
    </ListItem>
    <ListItem title="Database library design"
        >Inspired by the Haskell library <a
            href="https://github.com/3noch/postgresql-simple-interpolate"
            >postgresql-simple-interpolate</a
        >I created the Rust library <a
            href="https://docs.rs/postgres-named-parameters/latest/postgres_named_parameters/"
            >postgres_named_parameters</a
        >, which provides procedural macros to safely interpolate numbered query
        parameters (<Code>$1</Code>, <Code>$2</Code>, ...) using a more ergonomic named-parameter syntax.
        I like this approach because it provides the full power of raw SQL but
        without the usual pitfalls of manually numbered parameters.
    </ListItem>
</Panel>
